================================================================================
DEBUGUTILS KNOWLEDGE BASE
================================================================================
Files:  DebugUtils/Logger.h / Logger.cpp
        DebugUtils/CallLogger.h / CallLogger.cpp
Purpose: Runtime call logging for RVGL function hooks.
         Logger provides timestamped output to the VS Code Debug Console and a
         log file. CallLogger auto-generates passthrough hooks from a map of
         { RVA → name } with no per-function boilerplate.
================================================================================


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOGGER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Logger writes every message to two sinks simultaneously:

  1. OutputDebugStringA  — appears in the VS Code Debug Console in real time
                           when the process is launched or attached with cppvsdbg.
                           Select "Debug Console" in the Output panel drop-down.

  2. rvgl-randomizer.log — written to the process working directory, flushed on
                           every write so it is readable mid-session. When launched
                           via TestLauncher the working directory is the RVGL exe
                           folder (set in Launcher.cpp via GetDirectoryFromPath).


PUBLIC API
──────────

Logger::Init()
    Opens the log file. Call this as the very first thing in DllMain on
    DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH, before HookManager::InstallAll(). This ensures any
    log output from hook installation is captured.

Logger::Shutdown()
    Flushes and closes the log file. Call this as the very last thing in
    DllMain on DLL_PROCESS_DETACH, after HookManager::RemoveAll().

Logger::Log(message)
    Writes one line. A newline is always appended. Safe from any thread.

Logger::Logf(fmt, ...)
    printf-style convenience wrapper over Log.

Logger::TimestampLog(message)
    Prepends a full datetime prefix then calls Log:
        [YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.mmm] message
    Uses GetLocalTime() which fills a SYSTEMTIME struct of plain WORD (uint16)
    fields. No floating-point ops — safe to call from inside any hook including
    generic passthrough hooks where XMM registers must not be disturbed.

Logger::TimestampLogf(fmt, ...)
    printf-style convenience wrapper over TimestampLog.


FLOAT SAFETY
────────────

Logger is XMM-safe as long as you use integer format specifiers:
    %s  %p  %d  %u  %lld  %llx  %02d  — all safe, integer formatting only
    %f  %e  %g                         — UNSAFE, triggers FPU ops, clobbers XMM0-3

This matters because the x64 calling convention passes float arguments in XMM0-3.
If a hooked function receives float args and the detour calls Logger::Logf with %f,
those args are clobbered before they reach the original. Use %p to print float-
register arguments as hex if you need to inspect them.


DLLMAIN WIRING
──────────────

Logger lifetime must bracket everything else:

    case DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH:
        DisableThreadLibraryCalls(hModule);
        Sleep(50);
        Logger::Init();              // first
        HookManager::InstallAll();
        break;

    case DLL_PROCESS_DETACH:
        HookManager::RemoveAll();
        Logger::Shutdown();          // last

HookManager uses Logger but does not own its lifetime. Logger::Init and
Logger::Shutdown must not appear inside HookManager.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CALLLOGGER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CallLogger auto-generates passthrough logging hooks from a plain map. No
per-function detour or original pointer declaration is needed — just an RVA
and a display name.

Each registered function logs its name and all arguments on entry and exit,
then calls through to the original transparently:

    [2026-03-22 15:58:11.145] --> CreateCarEntity(0000000000000001, ..., 0000000000000021, ...)
    [2026-03-22 15:58:11.151] <-- CreateCarEntity = 0000000000000000


THE TEMPLATE POOL TRICK
───────────────────────

MinHook requires a unique function address per hook. You cannot install the same
detour on multiple targets because MinHook uses the address as a key, and the
detour body needs to know which slot it belongs to in order to look up the right
name and original pointer.

Solution: pre-generate N distinct functions at compile time using a template
parameter as a slot index:

    template<size_t Slot>
    void* __fastcall GenericDetour(void* a, ...) {
        Logger::TimestampLogf("--> %s(...)", g_slots[Slot].name, ...);
        void* result = CallOriginalSafe(..., g_slots[Slot].original, ...);
        Logger::TimestampLogf("<-- %s = %p", g_slots[Slot].name, result);
        return result;
    }

GenericDetour<0>, GenericDetour<1>, ..., GenericDetour<N-1> are all distinct
functions with distinct addresses. std::index_sequence expands all N
specialisations into a lookup table (g_detourTable) at startup. Registration
picks the next unused slot and assigns the corresponding detour from the table.

The pool size is compile-time fixed at MAX_AUTO_LOG_HOOKS (default 64, defined
in CallLogger.h). Increase this constant if you need more slots.


USAGE IN HOOKMANAGER
────────────────────

Call CallLogger::RegisterAll inside HookManager::RegisterHooks(), after
MH_Initialize() has been called:

    static void RegisterHooks() {

        // Typed hooks (when you need to inspect or modify args/return value)
        HookManager::Add(
            AbsFromRva(RVA_LOAD_VANILLA_CAR_POOL),
            reinterpret_cast<void*>(Randomizer::Hook_LoadVanillaCarPool),
            reinterpret_cast<void**>(&Randomizer::Orig_LoadVanillaCarPool),
            "LoadVanillaCarPool"
        );

        // Auto log hooks (call logging only, no modification)
        CallLogger::RegisterAll({
            { 0x0003FAC0, "LoadCustomCarPool"  },
            { 0x0004CB60, "InitPlayerCar"      },
            { 0x000F03A0, "CreateCarEntity"    },
        });
    }

Call CallLogger::UnregisterAll inside HookManager::RemoveAll(), before
MH_Uninitialize():

    void HookManager::RemoveAll() {
        for (const auto& entry : g_hooks)   // typed hooks
            if (entry.installed)
                MH_DisableHook(...);
        g_hooks.clear();

        CallLogger::UnregisterAll();        // auto-log hooks

        MH_Uninitialize();                  // free trampoline pool — must be last
        ...
    }

Do not register the same function in both RegisterAll and HookManager::Add.
If a function already has a typed hook, MinHook returns MH_ERROR_ALREADY_CREATED
on the second registration and does NOT write the original pointer. The slot's
original stays null and calling through it crashes. CallLogger::RegisterAll
detects and logs this case explicitly and skips the slot.


RVA CALCULATION
───────────────

All addresses passed to RegisterAll are RVAs (relative virtual addresses):
    RVA = Ghidra address − image base (0x00400000)

Example:
    Ghidra shows CreateCarEntity at 0x004F03A0
    RVA = 0x004F03A0 − 0x00400000 = 0x0004F03A0   ← NOT 0x000F03A0

A wrong RVA points MinHook at arbitrary bytes that are not a function prologue.
MinHook's HDE disassembler reads those bytes as instructions and can crash the
process before MH_CreateHook even returns. The VirtualQuery guard in RegisterAll
catches addresses that are not in executable memory, but cannot catch addresses
that land in the middle of a different function — always verify in Ghidra.


THE 8-PARAMETER CEILING AND WHY IT MATTERS
───────────────────────────────────────────

The generic detour is declared with 8 void* parameters:

    using GenericFn = void*(__fastcall*)(void*, void*, void*, void*,
                                         void*, void*, void*, void*);

    template<size_t Slot>
    void* __fastcall GenericDetour(void* a, void* b, void* c, void* d,
                                    void* e, void* f, void* g, void* h)

In x64 Windows the first four arguments are passed in registers (RCX, RDX, R8,
R9). Arguments 5 and beyond are passed on the stack at [RSP+0x28], [RSP+0x30],
[RSP+0x38], [RSP+0x40].

When a detour declares fewer parameters than the real function uses, the compiler
does not allocate stack space for the missing ones. The original function reads
those stack slots and gets uninitialized garbage instead of real arguments. This
produces the class of bug seen with CreateCarEntity:

    CreateCarEntity takes 6 params. The original 4-param detour left [RSP+0x28]
    and [RSP+0x30] uninitialized. The function read 0x21 from [RSP+0x28] as its
    spawnPosition float pointer, passed it to FUN_00510890 as param_2, which
    then crashed reading *(param_2 + 0x4) = *(0x25) — a near-null dereference.

Declaring MORE parameters than a function uses is always harmless. The callee
only reads the registers and stack slots it declares in its own signature and
ignores everything else. Extra parameters cost nothing beyond a slightly larger
stack frame per call.

The 8-parameter ceiling covers all known RVGL functions. If a future function
has more than 8 parameters, extend GenericFn, GenericDetour, and CallOriginalSafe
to match.


CALLORIGINALSAFE
────────────────

CallOriginalSafe wraps the trampoline call in a Windows SEH __try/__except block
so access violations are caught and logged instead of crashing the process:

    static void* CallOriginalSafe(GenericFn fn,
                                   void* a, void* b, void* c, void* d,
                                   void* e, void* f, void* g, void* h,
                                   const char* name) {
        __try {
            return fn(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h);
        }
        __except(EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_HANDLER) {
            Logger::Logf("[CallLogger] ACCESS VIOLATION: %s  "
                         "a=%p b=%p c=%p d=%p e=%p f=%p g=%p h=%p  code=0x%08X",
                         name, a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, GetExceptionCode());
            return nullptr;
        }
    }

Access violations are Windows SEH exceptions (code 0xC0000005), not C++
exceptions. A standard try/catch block does not catch them — __try/__except
is required.

MSVC does not allow __try in a function that contains C++ objects with
destructors (std::string, std::lock_guard, etc.). For this reason
CallOriginalSafe is kept as a plain C-style function with no RAII types.
The Logger::Logf call in the __except block is safe because it only runs
after the exception is already caught and the stack is being unwound.

On violation the function returns nullptr. This allows RVGL to handle the
failure through its own error paths (e.g. the "Can't create player" message)
rather than hard-crashing. The hook remains active for subsequent calls.


KNOWN LIMITATIONS
─────────────────

Float argument values cannot be printed from the generic detour. Float args
arrive in XMM0-3, not in the void* parameters. Printing them with %f would
require reading XMM registers explicitly (not possible in standard C++) and
would clobber those registers in the process. Print all args with %p (hex)
and interpret float values after the fact if needed.

Functions with more than 8 parameters are not fully covered. Extend the
parameter count in GenericFn, GenericDetour, and CallOriginalSafe if needed.

The generic detour cannot modify arguments or return values. For that, write
a typed hook in HookManager::RegisterHooks() instead.
